<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">Marcha Real</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">first printed <a href="/years/1761">1761</a>; Honour March <a href="/years/1770">1770</a>; official version <a href="/years/1908">1908</a> and <a href="/years/1997">1997</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The &quot;Marcha Real&quot; (Royal March), officially the &quot;Marcha Real Española&quot; and also called the &quot;Marcha Granadera&quot; (Grenadier March), is the national anthem of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, one of the oldest in the world.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In October 1823 Ferdinand overthrew the liberal government and repealed the constitution, and the <b>Marcha Real</b> became the national anthem again.</blockquote><a href="/songs/himno-de-riego">Himno de Riego</a></li><li><blockquote>Felipe said he had based it on three pieces: the &quot;<b>Marcha Real</b>&quot; of Spain, the Grand March from Giuseppe Verdi's Aida and &quot;La Marseillaise&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lupang-hinirang">Lupang Hinirang (Chosen Land)</a></li><li><blockquote>The &quot;<b>Marcha Real</b>&quot; (Royal March), officially the &quot;Marcha Real Española&quot; and also called the &quot;Marcha Granadera&quot; (Grenadier March), is the national anthem of Spain, one of the oldest in the world.</blockquote><a href="/songs/marcha-real">Marcha Real</a></li></ul></section>
